{"id": "swebq0157", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 1.81, "n_chunks": 2, "n_concurrent": 2, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.149, "eot_read_ms": 19, "reference_answer": "Belgium", "query": "where did flemish people come from?", "transcript": "Where did Flemish people come from?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Flemish people come from Flanders, which is the Dutch-speaking part of Belgium. They're a Germanic-speaking group with roots going back to early medieval settlers in the Low Countries. So today, \"Flemish\" usually means someone from that region who speaks Dutch.", "expert_answer": "Flemish people come from **Flanders**, the Dutch-speaking northern region of **Belgium**. Historically, they are a Germanic-speaking population from the **Low Countries**, with roots including **Franks** and other early medieval groups who settled in the area. Today, “Flemish” mainly refers to Dutch-speaking Belgians from Flanders.", "expert_latency_s": 2.27, "stall_ms": 20, "relay_ms": 1226}
